course: dictionary
lesson: 9373
title: Tiny cells that make new life
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A spore is a tiny cell that can grow into a new living thing. Fungi, ferns, mosses and some bacteria make spores, and each spore can start a new copy of its parent.
explain: Spores do the same job that seeds do in flowering plants. But a spore is much smaller than a seed, and it has no stored food inside it. «Fungi spread by spores, not by seeds.»
explain: Look at mould on old bread. «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» Spores from the air landed on the bread, and the fungus grew by feeding on it. You could not see a single spore without a microscope.
fact: A spore is a tiny cell that can grow into a new living thing.
fact: Spores are made by fungi, ferns, mosses and some bacteria.
fact: Spores do the job seeds do in flowering plants, but they are much smaller and have no stored food.
fact: «A mushroom is the part of a fungus that makes spores.»
fact: Ferns have spores on the underside of their leaves, and mould spores in damp rooms can make people with asthma ill.
exercise: «Fungi spread by spores, not by seeds.» What do fungi use to spread? | Flowers | *Spores | Roots | Seeds
exercise: What is a spore? | *A tiny cell that can grow into a new living thing | A type of soil | A flower petal | A large seed full of stored food
exercise: «A mushroom is the part of a fungus that makes spores.» What does the mushroom make? | *Spores | Leaves | Flowers | Roots
test: How is a spore different from a seed? | It is much bigger and full of food | It can only grow in water | It is made only by flowering plants | *It is much smaller and has no stored food
test: Where might you find spores on a fern? | Inside its flowers | *On the underside of its leaves | On the roots under the soil | In its stem
exam: Why do the spots of mould on bread appear in a few days? | The bread was baked with seeds in it | Bread makes its own spores when it is cut | *Spores from the air landed on the bread and the fungus grew by feeding on it | Mould grows only on cheese and never on bread
